A buffer overflow occurs when more data is written to a block of memory (buffer) than it can hold. This extra data overflows into adjacent memory, potentially corrupting or overwriting the values stored there. In the context of security, this can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, such as injecting malicious code into a program.

